1
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:04,880
Welcome to Launched.

2
00:00:05,120 --> 00:00:10,400
I'm Charlie Chapman, and today I'm excited to bring you the prolific Indie IOS developer, Ish Shabazz.

3
00:00:23,120 --> 00:00:24,240
Ish, welcome to the show.

4
00:00:24,560 --> 00:00:24,800
Hello.

5
00:00:25,280 --> 00:00:25,840
How are you doing?

6
00:00:26,160 --> 00:00:27,040
I am doing really good.

7
00:00:27,200 --> 00:00:27,600
And you?

8
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:29,520
Doing pretty good for 2020.

9
00:00:29,600 --> 00:00:31,040
Always like, perfect with that.

10
00:00:31,360 --> 00:00:31,440
Yeah.

11
00:00:32,320 --> 00:00:34,000
It's a low bar, but you've cleared it.

12
00:00:34,320 --> 00:00:35,680
For normal time, I'm horrible.

13
00:00:35,920 --> 00:00:37,680
But for 2020, it's pretty good.

14
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:49,520
So listeners of the show for a long time, or I guess from the very beginning, will know that I had Heidi Helen Polippus on as my very first guest.

15
00:00:50,080 --> 00:00:58,000
And that put me in a sort of a weird position because we talked about your recent apps on her show.

16
00:00:58,880 --> 00:01:05,360
And you have been on my list as a person that I just wanted to find an excuse to talk to and hang out with for a while here.

17
00:01:05,840 --> 00:01:07,040
And it was kind of weird.

18
00:01:07,280 --> 00:01:15,840
And so finally, the last couple of episodes I've done, we sort of ended up doing more of a like the launch of a person's career, so to speak.

19
00:01:16,400 --> 00:01:18,400
And I felt like those were just really interesting.

20
00:01:18,560 --> 00:01:25,120
And I liked hearing people's stories about how they got there just as much as the story of the specific app itself.

21
00:01:25,200 --> 00:01:26,160
And I was like, you know what?

22
00:01:27,040 --> 00:01:29,520
Who cares if it doesn't perfectly fit in with my model?

23
00:01:30,400 --> 00:01:40,800
I think I can finally bring Isha on and at least force him to hang out with me for an hour and get to talk to a person who's been influential for me as I joined this world.

24
00:01:41,120 --> 00:01:41,440
Thanks.

25
00:01:41,600 --> 00:01:42,240
That's awesome.

26
00:01:42,640 --> 00:01:45,360
So I guess let's just start from the beginning.

27
00:01:45,680 --> 00:02:30,240
I've actually I've listened to a couple of talks you've given where you sort of gave your introduction into the computing world and I sure it's a really powerful story to me so I just want to let you like talk about how you even got into this so I mean starting from where you grew up from the very beginning very beginning so I grew up in what is now called South LA in the 1980s which is what a notoriously kind of rough and and poverished area and I basically it was like a miserable time both at home and and at school and one day my teacher, misses Meade, came up to me and asked me if I could learn about anything in the world, what would that be?

28
00:02:30,960 --> 00:02:42,480
And I told her, I want to learn about computers, which is really weird because it's like 1986 and at that point in time I'm eight years old, but even adults, like, really didn't know much about computers.

29
00:02:43,200 --> 00:02:44,400
How did you know about computers?

30
00:02:46,560 --> 00:02:49,600
So it's really weird, just a random thing.

31
00:02:49,760 --> 00:02:55,360
I used to, like, oddly, I used to walk around the neighborhood a lot, which looking back, I'm like, what the heck am I doing?

32
00:02:56,160 --> 00:03:00,800
And there was like this, this store that sold, like, Casio keyboards.

33
00:03:01,120 --> 00:03:05,040
It could do all this fun stuff, like play more than just piano tones.

34
00:03:05,120 --> 00:03:07,760
It could play trumpets and flutes and all these other instruments.

35
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:08,320
Oh, okay.

36
00:03:08,720 --> 00:03:09,680
Like piano keyboards.

37
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:10,080
Yeah.

38
00:03:10,480 --> 00:03:12,560
And I was told, oh, it's computers.

39
00:03:12,640 --> 00:03:14,320
I'm like, oh, that sounds really cool.

40
00:03:14,400 --> 00:03:18,960
I kinda want to learn more about what goes into doing that.

41
00:03:19,440 --> 00:03:22,480
So to be honest, I really didn't know that much about computers.

42
00:03:22,640 --> 00:03:25,200
Only that computers could do this cool stuff.

43
00:03:25,920 --> 00:03:32,160
And I knew that computers were related to video games a little bit by the time, but weren't really that many video games even.

44
00:03:32,400 --> 00:03:34,000
But it just seemed like a neat thing.

45
00:03:34,240 --> 00:03:35,600
Like, oh, okay, computers.

46
00:03:35,920 --> 00:03:37,440
I want to know more about that.

47
00:03:38,800 --> 00:04:04,240
So what she ended up doing, and we didn't like speak any more about it for quite a while, but in the meantime, she ended up going to a class at the local community college, LACC, and she took a class on like computer fundamentals, basically like just, you know, how to boot it up and, you know, how to do simple things like that.

48
00:04:06,240 --> 00:04:40,480
She bought a computer with her own money and brought it into the classroom to teach me how to use computers and that was just a um a real transformative uh moment in my life um because pretty much since that point i spent my time doing like something computer related so in the um in the late 80s early 90s i was spending eight hours a day on computer this is before there's the internet right so looking back what was what was i doing What the heck was I doing?

49
00:04:40,640 --> 00:04:44,160
But I was spending like all my time just trying to like figure these things out.

50
00:04:44,480 --> 00:04:46,640
So that's kind of how I started.

51
00:04:46,800 --> 00:04:47,680
I ended up going to...

52
00:04:49,040 --> 00:04:53,520
a really weird story where I ended up starting college at like 14 and all this stuff.

53
00:04:53,840 --> 00:05:02,080
But when I graduated, I graduated with a degree in computer science and then nothing was super exciting to me in the computing world at that moment.

54
00:05:02,240 --> 00:05:03,280
This was in 2000.

55
00:05:03,520 --> 00:05:03,600
Okay.

56
00:05:03,840 --> 00:05:09,520
So this was like a little bit before text messaging came out and I decided I wanted to try something else.

57
00:05:10,000 --> 00:05:14,400
So I actually took a break from doing programming and such for a decade.

58
00:05:14,960 --> 00:05:23,600
The next time I looked into programming at all was ten years later in 2010 when the iPad came out.

59
00:05:23,680 --> 00:05:23,840
Wow.

60
00:05:24,480 --> 00:05:29,680
Steve Jobs stepped on stage, showed the iPad, and I was trying to talk myself into buying it.

61
00:05:31,120 --> 00:05:33,120
Just like I do, like, justifying the purchase.

62
00:05:33,200 --> 00:05:34,080
Like I do it with a HomePod.

63
00:05:34,160 --> 00:05:34,400
Exactly.

64
00:05:34,560 --> 00:05:38,640
So I'm like, I have three HomePods, but I probably need a HomePod mini, right?

65
00:05:38,800 --> 00:05:39,760
I mean, for testing.

66
00:05:40,320 --> 00:05:42,000
So who knows if users have that?

67
00:05:42,240 --> 00:05:43,200
For testing alone, right?

68
00:05:43,600 --> 00:05:51,440
And I was like, well, you know what, if there's like a daily planner app on the, on the iPad, because at the time I was really into, like, Franklin Covey planners and things.

69
00:05:51,600 --> 00:05:57,360
I'm like, if it had that, like, that alone would just, like, improve my life so much, I'd have to get it.

70
00:05:58,240 --> 00:06:00,240
It didn't come with one, but I was like, you know what?

71
00:06:00,400 --> 00:06:01,520
Okay., plan B.

72
00:06:01,840 --> 00:06:03,200
I can build one, right?

73
00:06:03,920 --> 00:06:05,840
I have a little bit of CS background.

74
00:06:05,920 --> 00:06:09,360
Now, granted, the programming I was doing before didn't even have a graphical user interface at all.

75
00:06:09,440 --> 00:06:10,640
It was all just like TechSpace.

76
00:06:10,800 --> 00:06:12,320
I'm like, ah, I could learn it though.

77
00:06:12,480 --> 00:06:13,920
Like, this would be like a fun thing to learn.

78
00:06:14,080 --> 00:06:20,400
So that was my entrance into IOS back in 2010.

79
00:06:20,800 --> 00:06:22,320
That's so wild.

80
00:06:22,560 --> 00:06:25,840
Like, so a ten year break from programming.

81
00:06:26,240 --> 00:06:27,120
Ten year break, yeah.

82
00:06:27,360 --> 00:06:29,040
And you could, you could jump back into it.

83
00:06:29,200 --> 00:06:30,560
Because I not at all.

84
00:06:31,280 --> 00:06:31,760
Not at all.

85
00:06:32,000 --> 00:06:33,200
I had to start completely over.

86
00:06:33,360 --> 00:06:33,840
I forgot everything.

87
00:06:34,000 --> 00:06:35,680
Well, but that's what I, but that's what I mean.

88
00:06:36,640 --> 00:06:44,560
It gives me so much anxiety to think like if I let up for a second, I'm just going to get so far behind that I'll never be able to catch up, if that makes sense.

89
00:06:44,720 --> 00:06:47,120
And I'm sure it was a lot of work, but it's at least possible.

90
00:06:47,840 --> 00:06:47,920
Yeah.

91
00:06:48,160 --> 00:06:48,960
Here's the thing about that.

92
00:06:49,280 --> 00:06:57,040
There are folks who were just born today who one day learn it and you have years of experience ahead of them.

93
00:06:57,360 --> 00:06:59,920
So you have all this stuff that you know that they don't.

94
00:07:02,240 --> 00:07:04,080
Like what it sounds like to fill a glass of water.

95
00:07:04,160 --> 00:07:06,880
Like there's just very basic things that you already like, like know.

96
00:07:07,200 --> 00:07:09,360
So you can always kind of jump back in and learn new things.

97
00:07:09,920 --> 00:07:10,000
Yeah.

98
00:07:10,400 --> 00:07:12,560
Well, and there's like these sort of foundational.

99
00:07:13,200 --> 00:07:17,920
concepts too that once they click in your brain, they just sort of sit there.

100
00:07:18,800 --> 00:07:22,960
There's someone I know who's starting to learn programming right now.

101
00:07:25,280 --> 00:07:28,080
She doesn't have any programming experience, but she knows Excel.

102
00:07:29,200 --> 00:07:43,280
It's really interesting how just having these kind of building blocks for putting values into some kind of block and that sits there and then it can serve something else up and data flows through these things this way.

103
00:07:43,920 --> 00:07:48,000
Just having that building block is so much like that was a huge thing to learn initially.

104
00:07:48,720 --> 00:07:50,000
But once you have it, for sure.

105
00:07:50,240 --> 00:07:52,800
You have this, like, stepping stone that moves you forward.

106
00:07:53,120 --> 00:07:54,240
So yeah, that's a good point.

107
00:07:54,640 --> 00:07:59,120
So one of the great disadvantages of me learning so early, I basically taught myself how to program.

108
00:07:59,840 --> 00:08:12,000
And I taught myself how to program using basic, not visual basic, basic basic, uh, where you have to put in like the line number, forget some of the line number before in like go to statements, right?

109
00:08:12,800 --> 00:08:17,920
I started on TI, uh, TI basic on our little calculators.

110
00:08:18,480 --> 00:08:20,480
So I sort of have some experience with that.

111
00:08:20,720 --> 00:08:22,560
Yeah, and that's that's a whole different world.

112
00:08:22,800 --> 00:08:37,200
So the thinking involved in like architecting like an app versus that, it's totally different in that you need to think kind of like top down as how things relate versus just working purely in the guth of a function kind of.

113
00:08:37,520 --> 00:08:37,680
Right.

114
00:08:38,080 --> 00:08:40,480
So yeah, it was interesting.

115
00:08:40,960 --> 00:08:47,760
There's those parts too, sometimes you can learn something and then the world just changes so much, you're like, all right, I'm just going to scrap that other thing I learned.

116
00:08:48,000 --> 00:08:49,440
Unlearning is almost the hardest part.

117
00:08:50,160 --> 00:08:50,720
Start over, right?

118
00:08:50,880 --> 00:08:58,960
So say you like, you got super good with auto layout and then you start using Swift UI, which does not use auto layout.

119
00:08:59,360 --> 00:09:00,240
So you're like, all right.

120
00:09:00,240 --> 00:09:03,600
All right, I need to think about how I approach this a little bit differently now.

121
00:09:04,080 --> 00:09:16,080
Well, and this is the thing I've heard lots of people talking about this recently, and it seems to be happening now live in front of me, which is when Swift came out, there was something brand new.

122
00:09:16,480 --> 00:09:41,760
And while like all of your, you know, UI kit, Objective C experience obviously would help you be better at writing Swift IOS applications, it created this like initial point where somebody could come in brand new and they could be pretty close to as good as somebody who's been doing IOS development for ament for a long time in at least Swift because we're all learning it from scratch.

123
00:09:42,080 --> 00:09:44,960
And like it creates, there's all these people that started with Swift.

124
00:09:45,200 --> 00:09:48,160
And I remember when Swift UI was announced, everybody kept comparing it to that.

125
00:09:48,560 --> 00:09:58,000
And I feel like in the year and a half or whatever that it's been out, I feel like I've seen that bear out, which is there's this slew of people who they just jumped on right with Swift UI.

126
00:09:58,480 --> 00:10:04,880
And they know it way better than I, a person who had six months of experience with iOS development before that came out.

127
00:10:06,320 --> 00:10:10,640
But even people who have been doing it for a while, they knew Swift UI better than a lot of us do.

128
00:10:11,040 --> 00:10:12,640
And it gives you this sort of.

129
00:10:13,440 --> 00:10:24,320
nice, uh, I don't want to say competitive advantage, but like, you know, you feel like you can be contributive early on, uh, compared to something that's super mature.

130
00:10:24,480 --> 00:10:27,200
Whenever you jump in, everyone else has all this experience before you.

131
00:10:27,520 --> 00:10:31,680
Was the iPad sort of similar, like, because you sort of jumped into the world as the iPad came out.

132
00:10:32,000 --> 00:10:37,760
So I jumped into the world as the iPad came out, but it was so similar that when I jumped in, it wasn't called iOS, it was called iPhoneOS.

133
00:10:37,920 --> 00:10:38,000
Yeah.

134
00:10:38,240 --> 00:10:43,840
And then there's this like switch of like, okay, so now let's kind of, let's split this up a little bit and make iOS.

135
00:10:44,000 --> 00:10:47,920
And then there's an iPad that was a separate thing than the iPhone.

136
00:10:48,000 --> 00:10:49,840
So you didn't have like the unified app.

137
00:10:50,160 --> 00:10:55,680
You'd have an iPhone app and then an iPad app and then yeah, it would so you'd have like two different storyboards.

138
00:10:55,760 --> 00:10:59,120
There's the iPhone storyboard and then the iPad storyboard that were not united in the same.

139
00:11:00,080 --> 00:11:08,000
It's interesting how things have kind of evolved like that and changed.

140
00:11:08,320 --> 00:11:13,760
So when I first started doing app development, Interface Builder was a separate app from Xcode.

141
00:11:14,240 --> 00:11:14,560
Oh really?

142
00:11:15,280 --> 00:11:15,360
Yeah.

143
00:11:15,600 --> 00:11:15,680
Okay.

144
00:11:15,920 --> 00:11:17,120
I didn't realize it was that recent.

145
00:11:17,280 --> 00:11:20,320
I knew it was like that in the 2000s, but yeah.

146
00:11:21,200 --> 00:11:23,680
So early on it was a separate app.

147
00:11:23,920 --> 00:11:38,080
So there's been a lot of little things like that, which it, one of the things I really enjoy about the IOS community, it's still pretty small, even though there's like tons of folks, it's still pretty small.

148
00:11:38,320 --> 00:11:46,160
It's small enough that if you decide you want to be an expert in pretty much any area of IOS development, you can be that person.

149
00:11:46,480 --> 00:11:48,960
Just focus on that, write blog posts on that, chat about that.

150
00:11:49,680 --> 00:11:50,400
You can be that person.

151
00:11:51,600 --> 00:11:55,360
And you can do it almost any time because every year we get new things.

152
00:11:55,600 --> 00:11:55,680
Yeah.

153
00:11:56,000 --> 00:11:59,360
For example, this year, like, who's the go to person for AppClips?

154
00:11:59,920 --> 00:12:02,240
I don't know, but someone could be.

155
00:12:02,480 --> 00:12:12,400
Like, there's a broad enough field out there that someone can pick up a new technology every year and just run with it and then be that expert even though you just entered the community.

156
00:12:13,040 --> 00:12:14,000
Yeah, that's a good point.

157
00:12:14,800 --> 00:12:16,080
I think that's unique and cool.

158
00:12:16,800 --> 00:12:18,480
Yeah, there was a small degree of that.

159
00:12:18,640 --> 00:12:25,200
I mean, it was very short lived, but I was one of the earlier people to really dig into the new widgets.

160
00:12:25,520 --> 00:12:27,120
Like, absolutely.

161
00:12:27,200 --> 00:12:27,280
Yeah.

162
00:12:27,680 --> 00:12:29,840
And again, everybody is way ahead of me now on that.

163
00:12:30,080 --> 00:12:38,000
But for this brief period of time right after it came out, I just happened to be set up where I could easily implement it and I had one of the first like test flight versions out there.

164
00:12:38,400 --> 00:12:47,680
And so it was funny because it's like, oh my gosh, like I feel like a legitimate person in this in this world because people are asking me questions and I actually have answers for them.

165
00:12:48,400 --> 00:12:49,680
And yeah, that was really cool.

166
00:12:50,880 --> 00:12:59,520
If you're a superhuman like Paul Hudson, then the day after Swift UI is announced you can write a book and immediately you're the expert.

167
00:13:00,000 --> 00:13:00,080
Yeah.

168
00:13:00,400 --> 00:13:07,120
Highlight of my career is Paul Hudson asking me one question about a thing I may have run into on widgets.

169
00:13:07,200 --> 00:13:09,680
And I'm like, am I like a peer in this moment?

170
00:13:11,360 --> 00:13:12,640
Yeah, it's it's it's really cool.

171
00:13:13,200 --> 00:13:21,440
that you can just kind of focus on a thing and then really, yeah, you can become the expert in it.

172
00:13:23,040 --> 00:13:26,560
So you decided you wanted an excuse to buy an iPad.

173
00:13:26,960 --> 00:13:28,480
And so you got one.

174
00:13:28,640 --> 00:13:29,920
It didn't have the app that you wanted.

175
00:13:30,240 --> 00:13:35,440
And so that was when you decided, I'm going to try and build this thing that I'm wanting personally.

176
00:13:36,400 --> 00:13:36,640
It is.

177
00:13:36,960 --> 00:13:41,040
And then I quickly realized, wow, this is way over my head for a couple of reasons.

178
00:13:41,280 --> 00:13:43,440
One, I haven't touched code in ten years.

179
00:13:43,920 --> 00:13:48,560
Two, the frameworks, while there's some stuff there, it wasn't really.

180
00:13:49,280 --> 00:13:50,720
ready to do what I wanted to do.

181
00:13:51,360 --> 00:13:54,960
So what I did instead was I made a PDF reader.

182
00:13:55,600 --> 00:14:03,920
Because shockingly, the iPad, this gigantic screen at the time, had no PDF reader.

183
00:14:04,480 --> 00:14:07,280
And I thought, what a waste of gigantic screen.

184
00:14:08,000 --> 00:14:12,000
So my very first app was a PDF reader, which I wrote in...

185
00:14:12,960 --> 00:14:25,600
a couple days because it was like a very simple simple app and I think it got approved maybe less than a week later and I was up on the store and got my first couple sales and I was over the moon.

186
00:14:26,240 --> 00:14:28,240
I think the first day I got like two downloads.

187
00:14:28,320 --> 00:14:30,240
I'm like, yeah, two downloads.

188
00:14:30,800 --> 00:14:37,200
Which was interesting because over time, in the beginning, I just figured that whoever downloaded my app is just like a friend of mine.

189
00:14:37,440 --> 00:14:37,520
Yes.

190
00:14:38,240 --> 00:14:38,560
Oh, yeah.

191
00:14:38,800 --> 00:14:43,760
This is just someone who I know and like, it's like, it's probably like my mom and like some of her friends or whatever.

192
00:14:43,840 --> 00:14:45,200
I'm like, okay, I'll take it.

193
00:14:45,440 --> 00:14:45,760
It's good.

194
00:14:46,320 --> 00:14:52,240
What's interesting is kind of as you grow, suddenly you start hitting numbers where you're like, okay, I actually don't know this many people.

195
00:14:53,360 --> 00:14:55,440
So maybe, maybe it's more than that.

196
00:14:55,600 --> 00:14:57,200
Maybe folks actually like what I'm making.

197
00:14:58,000 --> 00:14:59,360
That was exactly my experience.

198
00:15:00,080 --> 00:15:07,440
Like every single person who downloaded, for me it started with a test flight, but it was like, okay, this must be this person I know.

199
00:15:08,000 --> 00:15:12,960
And then it got to where it's like, okay, I don't think this is a person I know, but I think this is a person who I've interacted with on Twitter.

200
00:15:13,280 --> 00:15:18,960
And for some reason that felt like, oh okay, it's not as impressive because they're just doing it because they're being nice to me.

201
00:15:19,280 --> 00:15:28,160
But then there's a point where you're like, you get a service request, like an email support request from someone and you don't recognize the name and you go look it up and you're like, I don't know who this person is.

202
00:15:28,800 --> 00:15:34,640
It's like this incredibly joyful feeling, like this is a legitimate thing, like a real person is using this thing.

203
00:15:35,360 --> 00:15:36,000
Which is neat.

204
00:15:36,720 --> 00:15:41,600
The coolest one, I don't know if you've had this yet, is when you like see someone using your app in the wild.

205
00:15:42,080 --> 00:15:43,200
I I haven't had that.

206
00:15:43,440 --> 00:15:53,680
I've had like, every once in a while I'll search, you know, Twitter for my app name and I'll find like two people talking about it completely irrespective of me, not tagging me.

207
00:15:54,080 --> 00:15:54,160
Yeah.

208
00:15:54,800 --> 00:15:56,880
Somewhat tells me that maybe they don't even know who I am.

209
